#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161712 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "611af093-6621-5224-af2a-f769c7c0e691" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "054b9992-0bf6-5dcc-a04b-038ad6559959" :question/math-academy-id "e-8172" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075783/assets/ad112d0eeeea5affe9a7515bbe6e4b36fabc1f069b2152b7d29313936af644a5.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D=P^T \\! AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, find the third column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D=P^T \\! AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda_1=0,$ $\\lambda_2=1,$ and $\\lambda_3=4.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_3=4.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_3=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075783/assets/1b5ddaab68e99fda2cf15dbdcaaf5dfc8caca6b24ee6ea7b4d87e8fd49245c6e.png)\n\nwhere $\\| \\mathbf{v}_3 \\| = \\sqrt{6}.$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_3$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075783/assets/7c063e9aa7d59bf0f7b30cb311452508583b81d349076aa198cae059c038bb02.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161713 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b16e6cc-b1ee-5ccc-91a7-980bf5fe50a5" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "f6714653-0cfa-5049-9ae9-f921d46d03bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8173" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075783/assets/5cad03639230a7935a92ab107ec04502b0c8caa42c8fd72589b151d8da8fcd2e.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D=P^T \\! AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, find the first and second columns of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D=P^T \\! AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda_1=-1,$ $\\lambda_2=-1,$ and $\\lambda_3=8.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find eigenvectors that correspond to $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\qquad$ ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075783/assets/0f63b25d6d3527f5862ce68bea82cc1624630795d98226e144400da6bdc90ee4.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075783/assets/5be366991cc864298313b9b38f0a1cd252d7dbe8a237ab5d3cfe5e5e2f98eecf.png)\n\nNotice that eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075783/assets/7c24fd26d331372c96a26011c366aa8e3d51e88c2ac654d07c0d5fd377c672c2.png)\nand we find $\\mathbf{e}_2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{1}{5}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{e}_1$ and $\\mathbf{e}_2$ by their respective norms, we obtain the first and second columns of $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}_{1}}{‖{e}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{e}_{2}}{‖{e}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{15} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161714 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77a2b06b-e239-59a5-8af0-6cbbf27c3c08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "4779b6be-97c9-5389-b25e-e8f438f0a7fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-8175" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075783/assets/cc6da0a8cce346291ba1fd50fa1fe7fad0f79558e405dc521f9a7886ec035b41.png)\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3 \\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075783/assets/35d84ff9dbd4a7ea42873b716377487c3bb997386b76c29c3b3f1f0ae34218ae.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the third column must be orthogonal to the first and second columns, and\n\n- the norm of the third column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77a2b06b-e239-59a5-8af0-6cbbf27c3c08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce344a63-8e04-55eb-87e3-bff4525025bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160777} {:answer/id #uuid "327cf76c-975c-5e6f-978d-d9d7ab342db9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160778} {:answer/id #uuid "42d7b2d1-8355-5386-bed7-21c597a3eb64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160779} {:answer/id #uuid "4893cf95-c011-5122-94d1-4988010b724d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160780} {:answer/id #uuid "686e9341-7fcb-5b9c-a32a-de4976673d28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "686e9341-7fcb-5b9c-a32a-de4976673d28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160781} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e7843b6-3019-518e-a98a-66735a7efe8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2f119be-4dbb-5583-8e23-114609166369" :question/math-academy-id "q-93726" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\,∗\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the third column must be orthogonal to the first and second columns, and\n\n- the norm of the third column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77a2b06b-e239-59a5-8af0-6cbbf27c3c08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0d270e3-c061-5ce9-8328-37e01e5754fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382415} {:answer/id #uuid "45d63311-498a-5087-8c38-f5f44cdde578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382416} {:answer/id #uuid "ec11834a-5238-58c3-895a-abb7ddf4cbcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382417} {:answer/id #uuid "33dbfefd-5955-5981-80fa-c8047e2bb93e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382418} {:answer/id #uuid "5526ee36-d500-5e9d-b0ac-53690377ce9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0d270e3-c061-5ce9-8328-37e01e5754fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{al