& 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first and third columns, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "611af093-6621-5224-af2a-f769c7c0e691" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97d9ae48-0408-552a-a241-18bcf16ba094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382436} {:answer/id #uuid "26df6e0f-9703-55cd-869a-4d7875135031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382437} {:answer/id #uuid "1c19e650-d767-51d8-af9e-e4fc3a0e6e36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382438} {:answer/id #uuid "02a6b548-336d-5a70-8648-f4627742f230" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382439} {:answer/id #uuid "019ad869-da9d-51c2-bac4-c7aac854f7d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "019ad869-da9d-51c2-bac4-c7aac854f7d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382440} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd914366-82fa-58dd-a662-187a577dcf82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79bb35b5-15bf-5825-9442-a19c88e03d61" :question/math-academy-id "q-94257" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=0.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=0.$ Computing $A-0I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{3}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "611af093-6621-5224-af2a-f769c7c0e691" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfc4cdde-20bd-5f19-a3e1-f29d3d0d8877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382443} {:answer/id #uuid "460698dd-6094-5646-a8e7-9b01466d75ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382444} {:answer/id #uuid "d8cb7120-c478-5c9d-9cc1-8fa6692a2c2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382445} {:answer/id #uuid "12e2f1a8-af97-5e60-8706-24b83324951f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 